        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The Micro-6 payload using modeled microgravity (LSMMG, low shear modeled microgravity) suggested that C. albicans adapted to the environment of low shear by assuming characteristics consistent with increased aggressiveness. The goal of the ongoing studies, included in the Micro-8 payload, was to further examine the responses of C. albicans to microgravity by performing hypothesis-driven research in spaceflight conditions. The overarching hypothesis was that exposure of C. albicans to microgravity will alter gene expression and morphology, consistent with a potential increase in virulence. Notably, these studies were intended to 1) inform the value of LSMMG for predicting the physiological responses of C. albicans, 2) further explore and document the phenotypic parameters of C. albicans associated with pathogenicity and are altered during exposure to spaceflight, and 3) may predict conserved responses of higher eukaryotes, including humans, to spaceflight conditions.  As a pilot study to evaluate pathogenicity of the yeast, and beyond the scope of Micro-6, human monocytes were included as a mammalian host for infection in the Micro-8 payload. <p>

APPROACH:<br>
The Micro-8 study provided an opportunity to further explore and confirm the responses of C. albicans observed during Micro-6, including observation of cell behavior, cell morphology (shape), gene expression, and antibiotic sensitivity as well as a pilot virulence study. Specifically, the Micro-8 investigation included:<br>
1. A pilot study to more directly evaluate virulence using human monocytes as the host for yeast infection,<br>
2. Evaluation of the effect of spaceflight on physiological adaptations represented by filamentation and biofilm formation,<br>
3. Documentation of the differential expression of genes associated with the physiological adaptation responses.<p>

Two Group Activation Packs (GAPs) containing the monocyte experiment were launched into the ISS inside CGBA at +37°C and transferred to ISS at the same temperature. Fourteen GAPs containing the Candida albicans experiment was launched in a Double Cold Bag holding temperature at +22°C. Once at the ISS, these GAPs were transferred to two CGBAs currently onboard the ISS. The samples were held at +12°C until it was time to process the experiment.<p>

On Board ISS early operations include: Monocyte GAPs were manually activated by the crew and the experiment  was conducted at +37°C. At 100 hours post-activation, the experiment was terminated by the crew, transferred to MELFI, and held at -95°C or stored in CGBA at +4°C until returned to Earth. The GAPs containing Candida albicans were activated manually by the crew and processed at +30°C. The experiment was terminated manually by the crew 100 hours post-activation. GAPs were stored either in MELFI at -95°C, or within CGBA at +4°C, until return to Earth.<p>

RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is currently still being analyzed.  Full results will be available at the conclusion of the study.
        </div>

